1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,279
Can you show me image layers?

2
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:09,959
An image is not one big file.

3
00:00:09,959 --> 00:00:12,452
It is a stack of read-only layers:

4
00:00:12,452 --> 00:00:16,851
each step that changes files when the image is built (a RUN,

5
00:00:16,851 --> 00:00:22,129
COPY or ADD in a Dockerfile) adds one layer on top of the previous ones.

6
00:00:22,129 --> 00:00:25,648
A container adds one thin writable layer on top.

7
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:31,359
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

8
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:37,814
Every step of the build of the official nginx:1.30-alpine image is recorded:

9
00:00:37,814 --> 00:00:39,915
Read it from the bottom up:

10
00:00:39,915 --> 00:00:44,818
the Alpine base (ADD alpine-minirootfs…), then the nginx steps.

11
00:00:44,818 --> 00:00:51,744
Only the steps with a size are file layers; ENV, CMD, EXPOSE and LABEL are metadata (0B).

12
00:00:51,744 --> 00:00:59,760
<missing> only means those steps were built on another machine, so there is no local image ID for them.

13
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,439
Read it from the bottom up:

14
00:01:02,439 --> 00:01:07,431
the Alpine base (ADD alpine-minirootfs…), then the nginx steps.

15
00:01:07,431 --> 00:01:14,483
Only the steps with a size are file layers; ENV, CMD, EXPOSE and LABEL are metadata (0B).

16
00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:22,644
<missing> only means those steps were built on another machine, so there is no local image ID for them.

17
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:26,416
Let me try something.

18
00:01:26,416 --> 00:01:29,200
Build the broken/ Dockerfile.

19
00:01:29,733 --> 00:01:32,583
The history shows where the space went:

20
00:01:34,333 --> 00:01:41,173
Create, use and delete temporary files in the same step, so they never become part of a layer:

21
00:01:42,933 --> 00:01:43,647
Got it.

22
00:01:43,647 --> 00:01:46,398
What do I need to remember?

23
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:55,158
An image is a stack of read-only layers; RUN, COPY and ADD create layers, other instructions only metadata.

24
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:59,029
A container adds a thin writable layer on top.

25
00:01:59,567 --> 00:02:05,037
Layers are content-addressed (digests) and shared between images:

26
00:02:05,037 --> 00:02:07,646
stored, pushed and pulled once.

27
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:12,584
I keep hearing about pulling images.

28
00:02:12,584 --> 00:02:14,264
What is it, really?

29
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:19,225
docker image pull downloads an image from a registry.

30
00:02:19,225 --> 00:02:22,732
Every image name is really a full address,

31
00:02:22,732 --> 00:02:28,911
REGISTRY/NAMESPACE/REPOSITORY:TAG, and Docker fills in what you leave out:

32
00:02:28,911 --> 00:02:32,919
alpine:3.23 means docker.io/library/alpine:3.23.

33
00:02:32,919 --> 00:02:37,344
A pull downloads only the layers you do not have yet.

34
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:44,249
Show the images you have, with the digest each tag currently points to:

35
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,021
Pull an image you already have.

36
00:02:47,021 --> 00:02:52,250
Docker asks the registry whether the tag still points to the same digest:

37
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:56,477
Two results are possible, and both are useful to recognise:

38
00:02:56,477 --> 00:02:56,978
Status:

39
00:02:56,978 --> 00:02:59,557
Image is up to date for alpine:3.23:

40
00:02:59,557 --> 00:03:04,429
the tag still points to the digest you have; nothing was downloaded.

41
00:03:04,429 --> 00:03:07,437
429 Too Many Requests (or toomanyrequests:

42
00:03:07,437 --> 00:03:11,377
You have reached your unauthenticated pull rate limit):

43
00:03:11,377 --> 00:03:18,255
Docker Hub limits how many requests an anonymous user (an IP address) can make in a time window.

44
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:23,042
Two results are possible, and both are useful to recognise:

45
00:03:23,042 --> 00:03:23,546
Status:

46
00:03:23,546 --> 00:03:26,134
Image is up to date for alpine:3.23:

47
00:03:26,134 --> 00:03:31,024
the tag still points to the digest you have; nothing was downloaded.

48
00:03:31,024 --> 00:03:34,043
429 Too Many Requests (or toomanyrequests:

49
00:03:34,043 --> 00:03:37,998
You have reached your unauthenticated pull rate limit):

50
00:03:37,998 --> 00:03:44,901
Docker Hub limits how many requests an anonymous user (an IP address) can make in a time window.

51
00:03:44,901 --> 00:03:51,084
Even a pull of an image you already have counts, because Docker must ask the registry.

52
00:03:52,833 --> 00:03:54,555
Let me try something.

53
00:03:54,555 --> 00:03:57,753
Pull an image name that does not exist:

54
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:04,678
pull access denied … repository does not exist or may require 'docker login':

55
00:04:04,678 --> 00:04:12,216
Docker expanded the name to docker.io/library/cafe-menu-api:1.0, and Docker Hub refused it.

56
00:04:12,216 --> 00:04:20,251
The registry deliberately gives the same answer for "does not exist" and "exists but is private",

57
00:04:20,251 --> 00:04:21,742
so you check both.

58
00:04:21,742 --> 00:04:25,718
(An engine that pulls through a registry mirror,

59
00:04:25,718 --> 00:04:33,090
as CI machines often do, reports the same problem as docker.io/library/cafe-menu-api:1.0:

60
00:04:33,090 --> 00:04:34,250
not found.) 1.

61
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:37,920
Use the full, correct name.

62
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:42,471
A safe pattern for scripts pulls only when the image is missing,

63
00:04:42,471 --> 00:04:49,439
so it does not spend a registry request (or fail on a rate limit) when the image is already there:

64
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:51,913
Got it.

65
00:04:51,913 --> 00:04:54,664
What do I need to remember?

66
00:04:55,200 --> 00:05:03,974
A full image name is REGISTRY/NAMESPACE/REPOSITORY:TAG; docker.io/library/ is the default.

67
00:05:04,533 --> 00:05:10,628
A pull downloads only missing layers; a tag can move, a digest never does.

68
00:05:11,167 --> 00:05:16,548
pull access denied … does not exist or may require 'docker login':

69
00:05:16,548 --> 00:05:20,136
wrong name, private repository, or no login.

70
00:05:21,900 --> 00:05:25,064
How does listing and removing images work?

71
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:26,743
Images pile up:

72
00:05:26,743 --> 00:05:30,171
every pull and every build leaves one behind.

73
00:05:30,171 --> 00:05:35,274
This lesson lists them (docker image ls, with filters and formats),

74
00:05:35,274 --> 00:05:40,531
removes them safely (docker image rm), and cleans up dangling images:

75
00:05:40,531 --> 00:05:45,634
images that no longer have a name, typically left behind by builds.

76
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:51,359
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

77
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:56,443
Build the site twice, as two versions, and list the result:

78
00:05:56,443 --> 00:05:59,214
Useful ways to narrow the list down:

79
00:05:59,767 --> 00:06:02,661
Useful ways to narrow the list down:

80
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:06,505
A build without -t produces an image with no name:

81
00:06:06,505 --> 00:06:07,629
a dangling image.

82
00:06:07,629 --> 00:06:10,000
Dangling images also appear when you rebuild a tag:

83
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:20,782
the tag moves to the new image and the old one loses its name (with Docker's classic image store it is listed as <none>; the containerd image store,

84
00:06:20,782 --> 00:06:25,343
the default of Docker Desktop and new Docker Engine 29 installations,

85
00:06:25,343 --> 00:06:28,450
removes the old one when nothing else uses it).

86
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:32,020
Let me try something.

87
00:06:32,020 --> 00:06:38,954
Start a container from cafe-site:1.1, stop it, and then try to remove the image:

88
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:40,214
conflict:

89
00:06:40,214 --> 00:06:45,926
unable to delete … container 3c9d1b2f8a4e is using its referenced image:

90
00:06:45,926 --> 00:06:52,907
a container needs its image's layers, even when it is stopped (it can be started again).

91
00:06:52,907 --> 00:06:55,286
Docker refuses to delete them.

92
00:06:55,286 --> 00:06:59,649
Find the containers that use the image, running or not:

93
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:06,459
Remove the container first (if you no longer need it), then the image:

94
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:08,913
Got it.

95
00:07:08,913 --> 00:07:11,664
What do I need to remember?

96
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:17,325
docker image ls with --filter and --format finds exactly the images you need.

97
00:07:17,867 --> 00:07:23,751
docker image rm removes a name; the image is deleted when nothing else refers to it.

98
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:29,146
A container, even a stopped one, blocks the removal of its image:

99
00:07:29,146 --> 00:07:31,159
remove the container first.

100
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:35,260
Why would I need image tags?

101
00:07:35,800 --> 00:07:42,238
A tag is a movable name that points to an image ID, like a Git branch points to a commit.

102
00:07:42,238 --> 00:07:48,387
One image can have many tags, and docker image tag adds one without copying anything.

103
00:07:48,387 --> 00:07:50,557
Teams use this for versioning:

104
00:07:50,557 --> 00:07:58,225
the exact version 1.4.2 never moves, while the shorter 1.4 and 1 move to every new patch or minor release.

105
00:07:59,967 --> 00:08:03,926
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

106
00:08:04,467 --> 00:08:10,359
Build version 1.4.2 and give it the exact tag plus the two shorter ones:

107
00:08:10,359 --> 00:08:12,487
Three names, one image ID:

108
00:08:12,487 --> 00:08:14,369
tagging copied nothing.

109
00:08:14,369 --> 00:08:19,116
Now release the patch 1.4.3 and move the short tags to it:

110
00:08:19,667 --> 00:08:21,764
Three names, one image ID:

111
00:08:21,764 --> 00:08:23,620
tagging copied nothing.

112
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:28,300
Now release the patch 1.4.3 and move the short tags to it:

113
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:32,576
docker image tag to an existing name simply moves it.

114
00:08:33,133 --> 00:08:37,438
docker image tag to an existing name simply moves it.

115
00:08:37,438 --> 00:08:45,967
Whoever deploys cafe-menu:1.4.2 gets exactly that build; whoever deploys cafe-menu:1 gets the newest 1.x,

116
00:08:45,967 --> 00:08:47,673
whatever it is today.

117
00:08:49,433 --> 00:08:50,906
Let me try something.

118
00:08:50,906 --> 00:08:57,708
The team wants a second image, the "Menu Board", and names it the way it is written in documents:

119
00:08:58,267 --> 00:09:00,261
invalid reference format:

120
00:09:00,261 --> 00:09:03,133
repository name … must be lowercase:

121
00:09:03,133 --> 00:09:08,877
the repository part of an image name may only contain lowercase letters,

122
00:09:08,877 --> 00:09:12,148
digits, and the separators ., _, - and /.

123
00:09:12,148 --> 00:09:19,168
The tag may contain uppercase letters, but not +, / or spaces, and must not start with .

124
00:09:19,168 --> 00:09:19,567
or -.

125
00:09:19,567 --> 00:09:25,311
The same error appears in docker build -t, docker run and Compose files.

126
00:09:27,067 --> 00:09:30,041
Use a lowercase repository name:

127
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:32,513
Got it.

128
00:09:32,513 --> 00:09:35,264
What do I need to remember?

129
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:42,534
A tag is a movable name for an image ID; docker image tag adds or moves one and copies nothing.

130
00:09:43,100 --> 00:09:52,694
Exact versions (1.4.2) stay put; short tags (1.4, 1) move to new releases.

131
00:09:53,233 --> 00:09:59,196
Repository names are lowercase; tags allow A-Z a-z 0-9 _ .

132
00:09:59,196 --> 00:10:01,663
-, up to 128 characters.

133
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:06,844
What should I know about the danger of the latest tag?

134
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:11,037
latest is not magic and not "the newest version".

135
00:10:11,037 --> 00:10:15,861
It is an ordinary tag that Docker uses when you do not write one:

136
00:10:15,861 --> 00:10:20,611
docker run nginx means nginx:latest, docker build -t cafe-menu .

137
00:10:20,611 --> 00:10:22,467
creates cafe-menu:latest.

138
00:10:22,467 --> 00:10:27,810
Whatever was tagged latest most recently (or pushed most recently) wins,

139
00:10:27,810 --> 00:10:33,599
so the same command can run different software on different days and machines.

140
00:10:35,333 --> 00:10:39,293
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

141
00:10:39,833 --> 00:10:42,561
Without a tag, Docker uses latest:

142
00:10:42,561 --> 00:10:44,326
latest is only a name.

143
00:10:44,326 --> 00:10:50,423
You have two Nginx images, yet nginx alone does not refer to either of them:

144
00:10:50,967 --> 00:10:52,522
latest is only a name.

145
00:10:52,522 --> 00:10:57,894
You have two Nginx images, yet nginx alone does not refer to either of them:

146
00:10:57,894 --> 00:11:02,912
docker run nginx would therefore download nginx:latest from Docker Hub:

147
00:11:02,912 --> 00:11:05,881
whatever version that tag points to today,

148
00:11:05,881 --> 00:11:11,111
on a different base (Debian instead of Alpine) than the images you tested.

149
00:11:12,867 --> 00:11:14,324
Let me try something.

150
00:11:14,324 --> 00:11:21,471
A colleague builds a new version, also without a tag, and a second container is started "the same way":

151
00:11:22,033 --> 00:11:27,458
Compare the image each container was started by name with the image ID it really runs:

152
00:11:29,200 --> 00:11:33,929
Give every build an explicit version tag, and deploy that tag:

153
00:11:35,667 --> 00:11:36,380
Got it.

154
00:11:36,380 --> 00:11:39,131
What do I need to remember?

155
00:11:39,667 --> 00:11:44,171
latest is the default tag when none is given, not "the newest".

156
00:11:44,733 --> 00:11:50,468
It moves with every untagged build or push, so it does not identify what runs.

157
00:11:51,033 --> 00:11:57,593
Image IDs and digests identify content; exact version tags identify releases.
